WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.520
DNP3, or Distributed Network Protocol version 3,

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.440
is widely associated with electric utilities, water systems,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.960
and other geographically distributed operations.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.550
It supports telemetry and control across links that may have limited bandwidth or

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.030
intermittent connectivity,

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.141
including serial and IP-based networks.

00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:19.120
A DNP3 master, sometimes called a controlling station,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.980
communicates with outstations located near field equipment.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.605
The master requests data or directs approved operations,

00:00:24.605 --> 00:00:27.760
while outstations report measurements, status, and events.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.475
This model supports centralized supervision of assets spread across a large physical area.

00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:36.865
DNP3 can report changes as events rather than requiring every value to be transferred

00:00:36.865 --> 00:00:37.920
continuously.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.485
Event classes, time information,

00:00:39.485 --> 00:00:43.760
and unsolicited reporting can help a control center receive important changes efficiently.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.620
These features also give defenders useful behavioral context when collection and decoding

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:48.860
are configured correctly.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.795
As with other industrial protocols, deployment age and configuration vary.

00:00:52.795 --> 00:00:56.890
Traditional DNP3 did not assume the same hostile network conditions expected today.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.460
DNP3 Secure Authentication can provide message-authentication protections for selected

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:02.340
critical operations,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.680
but it is not a substitute for complete architecture, access control,

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.630
or confidentiality planning.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.565
Defenders should understand expected master and outstation relationships,

00:01:10.565 --> 00:01:13.475
communication paths, event rates, and operating windows.

00:01:13.475 --> 00:01:17.335
New masters, unusual control operations, unexplained communication loss,

00:01:17.335 --> 00:01:21.440
or changes in reporting behavior deserve investigation with utility and engineering

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.216
personnel.

00:01:22.216 --> 00:01:25.900
Protection combines segmentation, controlled remote and engineering access,

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:30.480
secure configuration, monitoring, time synchronization, and tested recovery procedures.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.150
DNP3 knowledge helps analysts describe what occurred,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:37.240
but process owners determine operational consequence and the safest response.
